          "title": "Correlation and Causation",
          "overview": {
            "text": "The lecture distinguishes correlation from causation and gives an example of a common cause that can produce an association.",
            "passages": [
              "p1",
              "p2"
            ]
          },
          "sections": [
            {
              "heading": "Key concepts",
              "items": [
                {
                  "text": "Correlation describes variables changing together; it does not by itself establish causation.",
                  "passages": [
                    "p1"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "A common cause can explain an observed association: hot weather can increase both ice cream sales and swimming.",
                  "passages": [
                    "p2"
